# Trivy suppressions.
#
# RULES FOR THIS FILE, read before adding anything:
#
#   1. Every entry needs a reason, an owner and an expiry date. An undated
#      suppression is permanent by accident, and permanent suppressions are how
#      scanners quietly stop finding things.
#   2. Suppress only what you have actually triaged. "It is noisy" is not
#      triage.
#   3. Prefer fixing. A suppression is a decision to accept risk, and someone
#      has to own that decision by name.
#
# NOTE ON THE BIG ONE: this project's base image (python:3.6-slim) is EOL and
# its dependencies are from 2018, so the scanners WILL report criticals. Those
# are deliberately NOT suppressed here.
#
# They cannot be fixed without rewriting the application, because Flask 0.12.2
# and PyYAML 5.1 do not run on a modern interpreter, and the vulnerable app
# code is the authorised target for Task 4. Suppressing them would hide the
# single most important finding in the repository.
#
# Instead they are handled by the fail policy itself: the blocking step runs
# with --ignore-unfixed, so unfixable findings are reported loudly but do not
# wedge the pipeline, while anything with an available fix hard-blocks. See
# task-2-cicd-supply-chain/README.md for the full rationale and the
# compensating controls from Task 1 that reduce their exploitability.
#
# Format: one CVE per line, optionally `CVE-ID exp:YYYY-MM-DD`.
#
# (No active suppressions.)
